Subject: [PATCH 0/2] media: i2c: GalaxyCore GC2083 sensor driver

This adds a driver and a binding for the GalaxyCore GC2083, a 1920x1080
10-bit Bayer sensor with a two-lane CSI-2 output. It is the sensor on
Milk-V's CAM-GC2083 module, the camera sold for the Duo and Duo S.

Supported: 1920x1080 RAW10 at 30fps from a 24MHz MCLK, two lanes, with
exposure, analogue gain, vertical blanking and flip controls. The driver
also accepts a 23.76MHz MCLK, the nearest rate the Sophgo SG2000 can
produce, and selects the link frequency from the MCLK rate.

Tested on a Milk-V Duo S (Sophgo SG2000), riscv64, with the CAM-GC2083 on
the J1 connector. The SG2000 CSI-2 receiver and raw capture drivers
(cv1800-csi2, cv1800-vi) are not in mainline. They come from
sg200x-camera-dkms, which the Armbian kernel for the Duo S builds in.
Results:

 - 1920x1080 RAW10 frames at 29.7fps from the board's 23.76MHz MCLK,
   which is 30fps scaled by 23.76/24.
 - The image is correct. A lit indoor scene comes out sharp with the
   right colours, which confirms the Bayer order SRGGB10.
 - Exposure and analogue gain work. libcamera's simple pipeline handler
   with the software ISP runs its auto exposure on these controls.
 - Changing a flip while streaming returns -EBUSY and succeeds after the
   stream stops.
 - Runtime PM really powers the sensor off: register reads NAK while it
   is suspended. Repeated module load/unload and bind/unbind cycles leak
   no clock reference.

Open questions, where I would like review:

 - Whether the flips change the image. The flip registers are
   write-only, and no frame has been checked with a flip set yet. The
   V4L2 side (the media bus code follows the flips) works.
 - Which parts of the vendor's init sequence matter. There is no public
   datasheet. The register table comes from GalaxyCore's reference
   settings in Sophgo's duo-buildroot-sdk-v2, and most of it is
   undocumented. Ingenic's GPL-2.0+ gc2083.c carries the same table for a
   27MHz MCLK and documents the PLL rates.
 - There is no test pattern control. The init table writes 0x008c and
   0x008d, which are test pattern registers on a related GalaxyCore part,
   but I could not confirm their meaning on this one.

There is no DTS patch. The sensor node needs a receiver at the far end of
its graph, and the SG2000 has no mainline CSI-2 receiver yet.

Based on v7.2-rc7. dt_binding_check and checkpatch --strict are clean,
and the driver builds without warnings at W=1 for riscv.

